{0 The value type and the reader}

Everything in [eon-edn] revolves around one type,
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value}, and one function that produces it,
{!Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value}. This chapter covers both, using the companion
example
({{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_edn/examples/book/reading_example.ml}[reading_example.ml]}).

{1 The value type}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value} is the whole AST — a plain, closed variant, no
abstract types to work around:

{[
  type value =
    | VNil
    | VBool of bool
    | VNumber of float
    | VString of string
    | VList of value list
    | VVector of value list
    | VMap of (value * value) list
    | VSymbol of string
    | VKeyword of string
    | VTagged of string * value
    | VMeta of value * value
]}

Pattern-match on it directly — there's no accessor API to learn. A keyword
like [:name] parses to [VKeyword "name"]; the leading [:] is stripped, not
part of the string.

{1 The reader}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value} is the grammar entry point;
{!Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run} supplies the low-level effects
({!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Peek}/{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Read}/
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Fail}) that drive it over a string:

{[
  let v =
    Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value
      {|{:name "Crab" :position [1 2] :alive true}|}
  in
  match v with
  | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VMap _ -> print_endline "parsed a map"
  | _ -> assert false
]}

This is enough for plain EDN — primitives, strings, symbols, keywords,
lists [()], vectors [[]], and maps [{}]:

{[
  match Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value {|(1 2 3)|} with
  | Eon_edn.Edn_effects.VList [ VNumber 1.; VNumber 2.; VNumber 3. ] -> ()
  | _ -> assert false
]}

[run] deliberately does {b not} handle {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Tag} or
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Meta} — parsing a document containing [#tag ...] or
[^meta ...] via [run] alone raises an unhandled-effect error:

{[
  match Eon_edn.Edn_parser.run Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value {|#uuid "abc"|} with
  | _ -> assert false
  | exception _ -> ()
]}

That's intentional: tag/meta resolution is a separate, composable concern,
not baked into the reader — see the next chapter.

